# 2026-09-21, surfaced by this PR's audit: `.claude/` had no entry, so every CLI
# `vercel deploy` uploaded this tree — agent settings and whole sibling worktree
# copies of the repo. Measured 2026-09-21 on one laptop: ~80,125 upload entries
# removed by this line. (An earlier draft said 82,537; three independent counts
# that day landed between 80,125 and 82,537, so the figure is a same-day reading
# of one disk, not a repo fact. Re-measure, never copy it forward — CLAUDE.md §5b.)
#
# [CORRECTED 2026-09-21 by this PR's own audit, security angle: an earlier draft
# of this comment said "No credential-shaped key is in settings*.json (checked),
# so this is build weight and noise rather than a leak." The check was real but
# it read the WRONG FILE — the glob matched the tracked `.claude/settings.json`
# (605 bytes; two keys, `hooks` and an `env` holding one numeric limit —
# genuinely clean, re-counted) and never opened the untracked
# `.claude/settings.local.json`, which is what Vercel actually uploaded, in 21
# copies. That file is NOT clean. This is the same tracked-tree-vs-disk mistake
# corrected six lines below, made again in the paragraph above it.
#
# Do not read this line as "nothing was exposed." What IS established: nothing
# under `.claude/` is ever SERVED — NEITHER vercel.json (root, nor
# apps/web/vercel.json) sets "public": true, and a read-only probe of
# /.claude/settings.local.json returns 404. So this was credential transit to a
# third party with retention, not public disclosure. Both halves matter; neither
# is the whole story. The founder was told the same day and took rotation and
# every repo-side remedy as his own. The record is the entry headed
# "Credentials in a public repo and in the Vercel upload" in
# .planning/v3.0-TECH-DEBT.md, committed in THIS PR — cited by its heading and
# not by its date, because that file already carries a DIFFERENT 2026-09-21
# entry and "the 2026-09-21 entry" would resolve to the wrong one. This line
# closes only the ongoing half: future CLI deploys stop re-uploading it.]
.claude/
# 2026-09-21: ANCHORED. The bare pattern plus a `!` negation (below) still
# excluded apps/web/src/pages/logs/ from a `vercel deploy` upload — the build
# failed with the exact "Could not resolve ./pages/logs/next/LogsNext" this
# file already warns about. Vercel's uploader does not re-include it the way
# git does. Anchoring costs nothing: both directories named `logs` in this
# repo are SOURCE (apps/api-gateway/src/logs, 5 tracked files;
# apps/web/src/pages/logs, 8) — both TRACKED ones, that is; the correction below
# names the untracked runtime dirs that exist on disk. `*.log` above still
# covers log FILES anywhere.
#
# [CORRECTED 2026-09-21 by this PR's own audit: an earlier draft of this comment
# said "there is no runtime logs directory". That is true of the TRACKED tree and
# FALSE of disk — and disk is what Vercel uploads, which is the whole point of
# the bug being fixed here. utils/logger.py:118-121 does a CWD-relative
# `Path("logs").mkdir(exist_ok=True)`, so both ./logs and
# services/agent-orchestrator/logs exist locally; the latter is 95 MB. Root
# ./logs is still directory-pruned by `/logs`, and today's single
# agent-orchestrator.log is still caught by `*.log` — but anchoring downgraded
# the NESTED dir from directory-pruned to contents-only, so a rotated .log.1, a
# .jsonl sink or a gzip would start shipping. The explicit entry below covers
# the one runtime path that actually exists, without shadowing source.
#
# [CORRECTED 2026-09-21, same audit, correctness angle: this sentence used to say
# the explicit entry "restores directory pruning". It does not. In the
# `ignore@4.0.6` that the Vercel CLI bundles, a trailing-slash pattern matches a
# directory's DESCENDANTS, not the directory itself — `ig.ignores(
# "services/agent-orchestrator/logs")` returns false. The uploader therefore
# recurses in and excludes the children one by one, and the directory surfaces as
# an empty-dir manifest entry. The OUTCOME is what was claimed (zero bytes ship,
# measured), and `datasets/` below already produces this exact shape on main, so
# it is known-good — but the stated MECHANISM was wrong, and this is a file whose
# only job is to stop the next reader being misled.]
/logs
services/agent-orchestrator/logs/
# 2026-09-12: the bare `logs` pattern above (for runtime log output such as
# services/agent-orchestrator/logs) also matched apps/web/src/pages/logs/ —
# a real page's source, not log output — and Vercel silently dropped it
# before build ("Removed N ignored files"), which surfaced as a rollup
# resolve failure on ./pages/logs/next/LogsNext with no other clue why. Same
# root cause already fixed twice in .gitignore (apps/api-gateway/src/logs/,
# apps/web/src/pages/logs/) — same fix here.
#
# [NOTE added 2026-09-21, round 3, correctness angle: that parenthesis names the
# two PATHS correctly but is over-precise about the MECHANISM for the web one.
# This file's CHALLENGED-AND-UPHELD note below credits "anchoring in
# apps/web/.gitignore" — but the root .gitignore ALSO carries
# `!apps/web/src/pages/logs/`, and its own comment claims that negation is the
# one doing the work. Two mechanisms, one named. The OUTCOME is settled either
# way (`git check-ignore -v apps/web/src/pages/logs/next/NEWFILE.tsx` exits 1,
# not ignored), and this file's actual subject is the Vercel uploader, not git.
# Recorded rather than resolved, because guessing which of the two is load-
# bearing is exactly the over-precision that has blocked this PR three times.]
#
# [This sentence was CHALLENGED and UPHELD, 2026-09-21, round 2 of this PR's
# audit. The correctness angle reported it false — "it was fixed once; there is
# no negation for the web path, and apps/web/.gitignore's bare `logs` still
# swallows it" — and produced a `git check-ignore -v` hit to prove it. That
# measurement was taken in a working tree sitting on a stale branch. On main,
# apps/web/.gitignore has been ROOT-SCOPED `/logs` since 2026-09-11 and its own
# comment says why. Re-run inside a worktree at this PR's head,
# `git check-ignore -v apps/web/src/pages/logs/next/NEWFILE.tsx` exits 1: not
# ignored. The sentence names the two PATHS rescued, not two identical
# mechanisms — api-gateway by a negation in the root .gitignore, web by
# anchoring in apps/web/.gitignore — and both were. Recorded here so the next
# reader does not re-open it, and because it is the SECOND time tonight that
# grepping this repo from a stale checkout produced a confident wrong answer.]
#
# [CORRECTED 2026-09-21, same audit: "same fix here" was wrong about WHICH fix
# worked. The negation below never did anything. Proof: apps/api-gateway/src/logs
# has no negation and behaved identically under the old file — both directories
# were pruned by the bare `logs` pattern before the uploader ever looked inside,
# so there was nothing left for a negation to re-include. ANCHORING is what fixes
# this. Removing the negation is a no-op, and the reason is mechanical rather
# than statistical, so check the mechanism and not my arithmetic:
#
#   node -e "const ig=require('<repo>/node_modules/.pnpm/ignore@5.3.2/node_modules/ignore');
#            console.log(String(ig().add('!apps/web/src/pages/logs/')._rules[0].regex))"
#   ->  /^apps\/web\/src\/pages\/logs\/$/i
#
# The compiled pattern requires a LITERAL TRAILING SLASH. The uploader tests
# paths produced by path.relative(), which never emits one, so this rule matches
# nothing it can ever be handed. The CLI bundles ignore@4.0.6, not 5.3.2 (see
# vercel/dist/chunks/chunk-SPXTKMOV.js:38861); its compilation differs in form —
# a lookahead rather than a bare anchor — but requires the trailing slash just
# the same. A full simulated upload walk with and without the line agreed with
# that (identical entry sets, ~3.2k entries each, under both the old and the new
# file), but treat the regex above as the evidence: it is one command, and the
# walk was a same-day script that is not in the repo. The line is kept only to
# hold this PR to one operation. That also means the
# 2026-09-12 fix recorded above never worked, and this PR silently repairs a
# second instance (apps/api-gateway/src/logs, 5 files) nobody had diagnosed.]
!apps/web/src/pages/logs/

